# Repository Boundary

## Purpose

Keep all remote I/O behind repositories so UI stays testable and the Supabase surface stays contained.

## When Applied

Editing data layers, or presentation code that fetches/mutates remote data.

## Requirements

- Remote API = Supabase Flutter SDK only (no Dio/`http`). Surfaces used inside the data layer: `.from(...)`, `.rpc(...)`, `supabase.auth`, `.storage.from(...)`, realtime channels.
- Flow: Page → BLoC/Cubit → Repository → (optional `*RemoteDs`) → Supabase.
- Multi-table/transactional work uses Postgres **RPCs**, not client-side multi-step writes.
- Map `PostgrestException` to user-safe errors (see [`../flutter/error-observability.mdc`](../flutter/error-observability.mdc)).
- Gold standards: `lib/features/auth` (interface + impl + `*RemoteDs`); `booking`/`sessions` for stream repos.

## Examples

```dart
final rows = await _client.from('sessions').select();     // read
await _client.rpc('create_booking', params: {...});        // multi-step
```

## Anti-patterns

- `Supabase.instance` in a widget/page (legacy admin scan/tracking/QR exceptions exist — add no new ones).
- Two sequential client writes that should be one RPC.

## Common Mistakes

- Business logic in the datasource instead of the repository.
- Skipping the repo and querying Supabase from a Cubit.
